    <briefdescription>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>This class is equivalent to std::atomic&lt;T&gt;. Some functions from the interface of std::atomic are missing - add them as necessary. Do not add operator= and operator <ref refid="table_8c_adec21d234619c8f0afbf0914249837b1_1adec21d234619c8f0afbf0914249837b1" kindref="member">T()</ref> <mdash/> it is better to make the code explicit about when and how loading and storing of atomic variables occurs.</para>
<para>The purpose of the class is that it performs far better than std::atomic for some implementations. For example the std::atomic in MSVC 2012 performs a compare-and-swap operation on a load even with the paramter std::memory_order_relaxed.</para>
<para>We force all the functions to be inline because they can contain switches on the value of std::memory_order. This will usually be a compile-time constant parameter so that after inlining the switch will disappear. Yet the code size of the switch may make some compilers avoid the inline. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
